\import Data.String
\import StringUtil

-- =============================================================================
-- Structural node paths, and the generated names derived from them.
--
-- The generator has no fresh-name counter: a name is derived from WHERE its node
-- sits in the λ□ term. A path is built by prepending one selector per step down,
-- so it reads from the node UP to the root, and distinct nodes have distinct
-- paths -- which is the whole uniqueness argument, provided the step from a
-- (kind, path) pair to an identifier is injective.
--
-- WHY A DATATYPE AND NOT A STRING. The path used to be a `String`, extended with
-- `"b" ++ natToStr i ++ path` and friends at ten sites, and names were spelled
-- `"p" ++ path` at five more. Injectivity was then a FILE-WIDE convention -- the
-- selector letters pairwise distinct, the name-kind letters pairwise distinct, and
-- no path ever starting with a digit so that `natToStr i` could not run into the
-- path behind it. Each of those is invisible in the branch that relies on it: a
-- new selector spelled `"c1"`, or a numeric selector without the delimiter, is a
-- silent miscompile, and the branch that introduced it looks fine.
--
-- Here the selectors and the name kinds are CONSTRUCTORS, distinct and injective
-- by definition, and `renderName` is the single place where a name becomes text.
-- Adding a construct means adding a constructor; forgetting to render is a type
-- error. What was left to prove is one lemma about one function -- `renderName`
-- is injective, so two names are equal only if they come from the same node and
-- the same kind -- and `NodePathUnique.ard` proves it. Nothing imports that
-- module, so it costs a normal compile nothing.
--
-- WHAT THE RENDERING GUARANTEES. `renderSel` emits a letter, and a selector that
-- carries an index emits digits after it, terminated by `_`. So the tokens form a
-- prefix code: the first byte says which selector, and a numeric one ends at the
-- one byte that is neither a letter nor a digit. `_` is used for NOTHING else
-- here, and a name always starts with its kind letter, so no generated identifier
-- is a bare `_` (reserved in Java since 9).
-- =============================================================================

-- One step from a node to a child. There is one constructor per (node kind, child
-- slot) in `compileExpr`, so a child's path determines which sub-term it is; the
-- indexed ones number the children of a node that has an array of them.
\data Sel
  | sFun               -- app: the function
  | sArg               -- app: the argument
  | sLamBody           -- lambda: the body
  | sLetVal            -- letIn: the bound value
  | sLetBody           -- letIn: the body
  | sScrut             -- case: the scrutinee
  | sProj              -- proj: the record
  -- compileFixMethod's second clause compiles an UNGUARDED fix body, and does it
  -- at the method's own path. Without a step of its own, a body that IS a lambda
  -- would mint the parameter name of that path a second time -- safe today only
  -- because `\elim` has already taken the lambda case, which is exactly the kind
  -- of reasoning-about-other-branches this type exists to remove.
  | sEta
  | sCtorArg Nat       -- construct: the i-th argument
  | sBranch Nat        -- case: the i-th branch
  | sMethod Nat        -- fix: the j-th mutually defined function
  | sWrap Nat          -- fix: the closure forwarding to the j-th function

-- A node address: the selectors from the node to the root, nearest first. Passed
-- down and branched per child, never threaded, so it costs the termination
-- checker nothing (see ToJava.ard's TERMINATION note).
\func Path : \Type => Array Sel

-- What a generated name IS. One per naming site in the generator; a node mints at
-- most one name of each kind, so the pair (kind, path) identifies a name.
\data NameKind
  | nParam             -- a lambda's, method's or forwarding closure's parameter
  | nLet               -- a letIn's local
  | nScrut             -- a case's scrutinee local
  | nThunk             -- a case arm's thunk parameter
  | nFixInst           -- a fix's local-class instance
  | nFixClass          -- a fix's local class (capitalized, as Java classes are)

-- The one byte per kind. Distinct letters; nothing else has to hold of them,
-- since a kind is one byte and comes first.
\func renderKind (k : NameKind) : String
  | nParam => "p"
  | nLet => "l"
  | nScrut => "d"
  | nThunk => "t"
  | nFixInst => "z"
  | nFixClass => "C"

-- A letter per selector; an index becomes digits delimited by `_`, so a selector
-- cannot run into the path behind it whatever that path starts with.
\func renderSel (s : Sel) : String
  | sFun => "F"
  | sArg => "A"
  | sLamBody => "L"
  | sLetVal => "V"
  | sLetBody => "B"
  | sScrut => "S"
  | sProj => "P"
  | sEta => "E"
  | sCtorArg i => "c" ++ natToStr i ++ "_"
  | sBranch i => "b" ++ natToStr i ++ "_"
  | sMethod j => "y" ++ natToStr j ++ "_"
  | sWrap j => "w" ++ natToStr j ++ "_"

-- The path as text. Also the node id in a runtime diagnostic (`Rt.unbound` and
-- friends, see JavaAxioms.ard), which is why it is readable at all: the root is
-- the empty string, and reading left to right walks from the node to the root.
\func renderPath (p : Path) : String \elim p
  | nil => ""
  | s :: p => renderSel s ++ renderPath p

-- The only way a name becomes text.
\func renderName (k : NameKind) (p : Path) : String => renderKind k ++ renderPath p
